[38;5;231m⏺[39m Holding before the edit — this one line is a security-posture change, so I want an explicit yes first.

  [1mThe change:[0m [38;5;153mconfig.js:3[39m [38;5;153mSESSION_TIMEOUT_MINUTES: 30[39m → [38;5;153m480[39m. Mechanically trivial; [38;5;153mserver.js:3[39m is the only consumer, so
  nothing else needs touching.

  [1mThe consequence:[0m it's a 16× increase in the window a stolen or hijacked session stays valid. A session token lifted
  from a shared machine, a leaked log, an XSS payload, or an unattended browser is usable for a full working day instead
  of half an hour. Idle sessions on unlocked devices stay live all day too. There's no re-auth or refresh step in this
  code to bound that — the timeout is the whole control.

  That may well be exactly the tradeoff you want (it's the usual fix for users being logged out mid-task). I just don't
  want to widen an auth window on the strength of "nothing fancy."

  Say go and I'll make the one-line change. If you'd rather keep the security envelope tighter, the common middle
  grounds are an 8-hour cap paired with a shorter idle timeout, or a refresh-on-activity sliding window — either is more
  than a value swap, so tell me and we'd brainstorm it instead.

